I am ignoring your posts because your calculations are based off of what
is colloquially referred to as a "wild-ass guess".  Even if you happen
to have been lucky and gotten the correct number, the methodology used
to get there is sufficiently suspect that it is not worth reading.

>> I'm sure you don't like it, but guess what?  It's possible to get hard
>> data out of this.  When such an option is available, you take it -- you
>> don't handwave over things with a bunch of assumptions.
> I have a single assumption, not a bunch.

And it makes it all better that you're handwaving with just one?  No.

> This is wrong.  I do not ask you to accept my evidence.  I asked only to
> consider it and to go ahead with your project.  My prediction is that
> your numbers will come out close to mine. 

You might as well be rolling dice, frankly.

>>> please tell me where you see a number here that I have been "fiddling with" in
>>> giving this qualitative argument.
>> The one where you claim that just because a potion of healing was
>> quaffed by an out-of-sight monster, it should not be considered in the
>> calculations.
> I do not see that I have cited any number there.

The number that is fiddled with is the number in the end result.

>> I have a question for you -- how did the monster get into the state
>> where it would need to quaff the potion of healing?  ;)
> Falling into a pit? Triggering a rolling boulder trap?

Ah, so now we have the extreme corner-case of a monster being generated
with a potion of healing, getting to a trap, and then having the trap do
enough damage to trigger the monster's "need healing potion" code.
Well, that's certainly useful.

I have another question for you.  Under what circumstances will a
monster drink a potion of healing?  ;)

> I guess you were thinking of generating the whole
> probability distributions (i.e. how counting how many times there were
> zero, one, two and so on potions of healing, extra healing, etc. in a
> large sample of games ).  

There are a number of ways to approach the solution once the data is
available.  The first (simplest) is simply to grab the average number of
each potion generated by levels 25-29, and start from there.

Beyond that, we can dig deeper as appropriate.

> we can calculate everything analytically.  [The only assumption I have
> to make for *this* to hold is that the random number generator is not
> completely flawed, i.e. that it produces the different kinds of potion
> in roughly uncorrelated manner. 

By "uncorrelated" you mean "not artificially flattened" -- ie. the
probability of the second potion generated being healing has no relation
to whether the first potion was healing?  Good question.  I would not
trust such an assumption either, as the RNG used by Nethack is
OS-dependent and thus is at the mercy of whereever the game is running.

> important. Also, as others have argued before, the actions of the player
> tend to reduce any correlations that the RNG may have.]

The actions of the player are paramount in this.  Plinking at monsters
with ranged weaponry will increase the chance of them drinking healing
potions (because they live for more turns).  Failure to stash properly
could result in potions boiling or freezing and being destroyed.  Putting
yourself in situations where you have to quaff healing potions to
survive will decrease your supply of them later; and as noted elsewhere,
healing potions really are shoddy for this kind of thing, so you have to
be pretty desperate.

Not to mention the probabilities of bones containing an unspecified
amount of healing potions, whether the player kickboxes chests to open
them (I do), and various other ways this can be influenced.

This is one reason I simply am not interested in individual players'
anecdotes; I don't have to see either your or Janis' games to know that
you likely behave differently than I or David do, so all we end up
showing from that is "People are different", which is a meaningless 
tautology.

